      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the advice. i think i'll go with the 2x12 to be safe. what about the post-to-beam connections? are there specific types of hardware or structural issues i should watch out for so the deck stays stable and doesn't shift or rot over time?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That is a wise decision, and I must admit, choosing the 2x12 is a move that will make the deck feel incredibly sturdy, much like a well-organized storage room where every item has its place and nothing is left to chance.\n\nRegarding those post-to-beam connections, you absolutely cannot skimp on the hardware, because if you use standard nails or weak brackets, the entire structure could shift or even collapse under the weight of the snow, which is a very serious risk. You should definitely look into using galvanized steel post bases and ledger brackets specifically rated for the Vancouver snow loads, as these are designed to handle the pressure without failing.\n\nIt is also crucial to ensure that the connection points are sealed properly with a high-quality sealant or flashing, because if water gets trapped in the wood between the metal and the cedar, it will rot the connection point very quickly, and you don't want your deck to start leaking or shifting after just a few winters.\n\nFurthermore, make sure to use structural screws or bolts rather than just nails, as these provide a much stronger hold and are less likely to loosen over time due to the vibration of the wind or the settling of the ground.\n\nFinally, remember that even",
